<TopicPage slug="subjects/design/expert/design-strategy-and-leadership">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Design strategy is the ladder\'s summit: <strong>design as a business capability led, funded
    and measured</strong> — principles instead of pixels, systems instead of screens, and teams
    built to compound. It is where every previous design lesson becomes a decision someone makes
    deliberately.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="leverage, the toolkit, organisational design, the AI inflection" widget="equationStep" config={stratCfg} />

  <h2>The leader\'s evidence base</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Design\'s business case, documented:</strong> McKinsey\'s Design Index and the Design Management Institute\'s studies associated design-led firms with superior returns — correlational, caveated (the statistics lesson\'s discipline), but directionally consistent: design quality is measurable and it tracks outcomes.</li>
    <li><strong>Principles beat opinion at scale:</strong> a written principle ("progressive disclosure everywhere") settles debates without seniority contests — the argument from principle is the strategy lesson\'s positioning, internalised; the best design orgs are legible from their principles.</li>
    <li><strong>Portfolio careers need the whole ladder:</strong> the generalist-expert tension (the scaling lesson) resolves as T-shaped (deep in one, conversant across) — the breadth this atlas itself models.</li>
    <li><strong>Where this ladder ends:</strong> strategy closes the loop the design lesson opened — deciding what deserves to exist, then using every lesson above to make it excellent. The craft was never the ceiling; it was the foundation.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> McKinsey Design Index; "Designing Design Organizations" (Org Design for Design Orgs); the business, strategy and AI-alignment lessons.</p>
</TopicPage>
